    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm working on a 12 week hypertrophy block for a client but they've completely stalled on their squat for the last 3 weeks. progress just stopped. can you explain how deloading or changing the volume/intensity might help them break through this plateau from a physiological standpoint?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A plateau often signals that your body needs a break or a change in stimulus. Deloading reduces training stress, allowing the nervous system and muscle tissue to recover fully from accumulated fatigue, which can facilitate strength gains upon return. Alternatively, manipulating volume or intensity—like switching from heavy sets to higher reps, or vice versa—can shock the system and target different muscular adaptations, prompting new levels of growth. Physiologically, this breaks the familiar pattern your body has adapted to, forcing it to adapt again to the new demands.",
      "source": "target",
