    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i was thinking about the CNS side of things too. how much does systemic fatigue actually play into this? like is it just about the muscles being tired, or is there something more deep-seated happening with the neurological drive that makes it feel like they've just hit a wall?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Systemic fatigue involves much more than just muscle soreness; it significantly taxes the Central Nervous System (CNS). When the CNS is overwhelmed by consistent high-intensity loading, it can downregulate the motor unit recruitment needed for maximum force production, leading to that \"hitting a wall\" feeling. Essentially, the brain becomes less efficient at signaling the muscles to fire optimally, creating a perceived strength barrier even if the muscle tissue itself isn't completely exhausted.",
      "source": "target",
